--- body ---
## Context
Zotero 10 ships a finished keyword index that zoteus does not use. The
platform facts are verified and recorded in SPEC.md §4 C2; this ticket
decides what, if anything, we do about them.

What is on disk today, measured on the author's machine 2026-08-29:
`fulltext.sqlite`, four contentless FTS5 tables, 13 090 content documents.
`fulltextContent.rowid` is the local `itemID`, joined 13 090 of 13 090. A
read-only open of the live file answered a count in 7 ms and a `MATCH` in
8 ms with Zotero running.

What zoteus does instead: it reads `/items/<key>/fulltext` and
`/fulltext?since=` over the local API and builds its own keyword index from
the text. That is why the Zotero 10 move did not break it, where the field
review found three projects broken by reading `zotero.sqlite` directly.

So the keyword half of retrieval may already exist, for free, with no
extraction pass, no build time and no memory budget. That is the gate.

## What makes it a real question and not an obvious win
Three things cut against it, and any recommendation has to answer all three.

**The index locates a term, not an entry.** The tables are contentless, so
the stored column and `snippet()` return nothing and a document cannot be
printed back. That much is measured and stands. What does NOT stand is the
bound this ticket first drew from it, that the index can serve "which items,
never which passage": the postings survive, `fts5vocab(…, 'instance')`
constrained by term returns `(itemID, token offset)` in under a millisecond,
and a query term therefore does locate itself inside an item (log,
2026-08-29; SPEC.md §4 C2 corrected to match). The bound that survives is
narrower and still binding. Our unit of answer is the entry (ratified
2026-08-26), an entry is a span where an offset is a point, and turning a
token offset into a character position requires reproducing Zotero's own
tokenization exactly — an approximation drifted +13, +2 and 0 tokens on three
documents. So the index can serve part of the keyword path, not the path.

**It is undocumented and it moves.** No release note names `fulltext.sqlite`,
FTS5, or the split; the 10.0 changelog says only "Much faster full-text
content searches". It already moved once, silently, at userdata step 127.
Depending on an internal file is exactly the exposure C2 exists to describe.

**Concurrency is weaker than ours.** `journal_mode` is `delete`, not WAL, so
a writer takes an exclusive lock. Reads are cheap but not guaranteed
available, and we do not control when Zotero writes.

## Actions
1. Establish what a hybrid would actually buy. Measure our own keyword build
   (wall time, peak RSS, disk) against zero for the platform path, on the
   same library. A saving nobody measured is not a saving.
2. Determine what the platform index cannot answer that R-items require.
   Work from SPEC.md §3, not from intuition: entry-level location (the
   ratified unit), coverage honesty (R4, R17), the filters, accent and CJK
   behaviour. Anything on that list the index cannot serve is a hard bound
   on how much of the keyword path it can take.
3. Test availability under real write load. Run reads while Zotero indexes a
   large attachment and record failures and latencies. A positive control is
   required: force a writer to hold the lock and confirm the probe reports
   the failure rather than silently returning stale or empty.
4. Compare the tokenizers. `fulltextContent` is unicode61 where ours is
   configured for our own folding rules; state where the two disagree, since
   a difference changes which queries match and is not a performance detail.
5. Price the coupling. If we read it, name what breaks when Zotero moves it
   again, and how we would detect that before a user does. SYNC.md's watch
   on `fulltext.js` and `userdata.sql` (ticket 0060) is the detection half.
6. Weigh the SDT pack as the other platform substrate, because it answers
   what the index cannot. `.zotero-sdt-cache` carries `anchor.pageRects` per
   block and a per-glyph `textMap` per run, which is entry-level location on
   the page, and a source hash plus processor version, so staleness is
   detectable where the `.zotero-ft-cache` generations give no signal at all.
   Read one with `verification/probes/sdt_read.py`. Its bound is availability rather than
   quality: a pack exists only for an attachment opened in Zotero's reader,
   2 of 13 631 on the author's machine at filing. Say what would have to
   change for that to stop being decisive, and price the coupling as in 5 —
   the surface is watched in SYNC.md, and a processor bump regenerates packs
   silently.
7. Recommend, with the reasoning: use the index, use the pack, ignore both,
   or use either behind a fallback that rebuilds our own when the file is
   absent, locked, or schema-shifted.

## Test
An experiment, not a unit test. Build the comparison in the bench harness so
the numbers land in `bench/results/` and can be declared in the figure
guard. Every figure quoted in prose from this work gets an anchor.

## Verification
- [ ] The saving is measured on the real library, not estimated
- [ ] Every R-item the platform index cannot serve is named
- [ ] The availability probe has a positive control that fires
- [ ] The recommendation states what breaks on the next platform move

## Invariants
- The entry as unit of answer is ratified and not reopened here. If the
  platform index cannot serve it, that bounds the ticket's scope; it does
  not reopen the ruling.
- Coverage honesty (R4, R17) survives whatever is decided. An index we did
  not build is still an index we must report on truthfully, including its
  staleness, which we do not control.
- C1 holds: anything derived from the platform index is derived data and
  carries a key, the platform's own `localUserKey` included.
- Nothing here is filed upstream. The PR budget is untouched.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The measured comparison exists in bench/results/
- [ ] The bound (what it cannot answer) is stated against SPEC.md §3
- [ ] Availability under write load is measured, with a positive control
- [ ] The SDT branch is answered, not skipped: what the pack can serve that
      the index cannot, and what its availability bound costs
- [ ] A recommendation is recorded, and if it changes the design it goes to
      DECISIONS.md first
